train.f01:
Assumptions: the evaluator calls an OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint (env vars OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_BASE_URL, MODEL). The 10 tickets are split 7 train / 3 held-out, stratified across the four labels where possible.

```json
{
  "seed_candidate": {
    "prompt": "Classify the support ticket."
  },
  "evaluator": {
    "type": "python",
    "code": "import json, os, re, urllib.request

LABELS = ['billing', 'bug', 'feature_request', 'account']

def call_model(system, user):
    key = os.environ.get('OPENAI_API_KEY', '')
    base = os.environ.get('OPENAI_BASE_URL', 'https://api.openai.com/v1').rstrip('/')
    model = os.environ.get('MODEL', 'gpt-4o-mini')
    body = json.dumps({'model': model, 'temperature': 0, 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': system}, {'role': 'user', 'content': user}]}).encode()
    req = urllib.request.Request(base + '/chat/completions', data=body, headers={'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + key})
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30) as r:
        data = json.loads(r.read().decode())
    return data['choices'][0]['message']['content']

def evaluate(candidate, example):
    text = candidate if isinstance(candidate, str) else next(iter(candidate.values()), '')
    if example is None:
        example = {'input': 'I was charged twice', 'label': 'billing'}
    inp = example.get('input', '')
    expected = example.get('label', example.get('expected', ''))
    try:
        got = call_model(text, inp)
    except Exception as e:
        return 0.0, {'input': inp, 'expected': expected, 'error': repr(e)}
    reply = (got or '').strip().lower()
    found = [l for l in LABELS if re.search(r'\b' + re.escape(l) + r'\b', reply)]
    exact = reply.strip(' ."\'`*') == expected
    if exact:
        score = 1.0
    elif found == [expected]:
        score = 0.7
    else:
        score = 0.0
    return score, {'input': inp, 'expected': expected, 'got': got, 'labels_mentioned': found, 'failing_checks': [] if exact else ['output_not_exactly_label' if found == [expected] else 'wrong_or_ambiguous_label']}
",
    "timeout": 60
  },
  "objective": "Maximize routing accuracy (reply must be exactly one label from: billing, bug, feature_request, account, with no extra text) on held-out tickets. Full credit only for an exact label-only reply; partial credit if the correct label is the only label mentioned amid extra text. Keep the prompt under 250 words and do not embed the specific example tickets verbatim.",
  "background": "Ticket routing prompt. Categories inferred from labels: billing (charges, refunds, invoices, payment methods), bug (something broken or behaving incorrectly), feature_request (asking for new capability), account (login, password, email, access). Assumes an OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint configured through OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_BASE_URL and MODEL env vars; the candidate is the system message and the ticket text the user message. The seed prompt does not list the labels, so the optimizer must add them. The 10 labelled tickets were split 7 train / 3 validation; this is a small, noisy validation set, so generalization estimates are rough. Examples use the key 'label' for the expected value.",
  "dataset": [
    {"input": "I was charged twice for my March invoice", "label": "billing"},
    {"input": "App crashes when I upload a PNG over 5MB", "label": "bug"},
    {"input": "Can you add SSO with Okta?", "label": "feature_request"},
    {"input": "How do I reset my password?", "label": "account"},
    {"input": "Refund the annual plan, I cancelled in week one", "label": "billing"},
    {"input": "Export to CSV drops the last row", "label": "bug"},
    {"input": "Please support dark mode in the editor", "label": "feature_request"}
  ],
  "valset": [
    {"input": "I can't log in after changing my email", "label": "account"},
    {"input": "Your card on file was declined, why?", "label": "billing"},
    {"input": "Search returns results from deleted projects", "label": "bug"}
  ],
  "config": {"max_metric_calls": 50, "max_iterations": 12}
}
```

train.f02:
I split gold.jsonl 6 train / 2 held-out (the Dr. Chen title/acronym case and the NASA possessive case). With only 8 examples, the validation score will be noisy. I assumed the evaluator calls an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, since you didn't say how you run the prompt.

```json
{
  "seed_candidate": {
    "extract_prompt": "Extract the people, organizations and locations from the text as JSON.
"
  },
  "evaluator": {
    "type": "python",
    "timeout": 90,
    "code": "import json, os, re, urllib.request

KEYS = ['person', 'org', 'location']

def f1(p, g):
    p = set(p)
    g = set(g)
    if not p and not g:
        return 1.0
    tp = len(p & g)
    if tp == 0:
        return 0.0
    pr = tp / len(p)
    rc = tp / len(g)
    return 2 * pr * rc / (pr + rc)

def evaluate(candidate, example):
    text = candidate if isinstance(candidate, str) else next(iter(candidate.values()), '')
    if example is None:
        example = {'input': 'Priya Nair joined Stripe in Dublin.', 'expected': {'person': ['Priya Nair'], 'org': ['Stripe'], 'location': ['Dublin']}}
    inp = example.get('input', '')
    exp = example.get('expected', {})
    reply = ''
    try:
        key = os.environ.get('OPENAI_API_KEY', '')
        base = os.environ.get('OPENAI_BASE_URL', 'https://api.openai.com/v1').rstrip('/')
        model = os.environ.get('MODEL', 'gpt-4o-mini')
        body = json.dumps({'model': model, 'temperature': 0, 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': text}, {'role': 'user', 'content': inp}]}).encode()
        req = urllib.request.Request(base + '/chat/completions', data=body, headers={'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + key})
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=60) as r:
            reply = json.loads(r.read().decode())['choices'][0]['message']['content']
    except Exception as e:
        return 0.0, {'input': inp, 'error': 'request failed: ' + repr(e)}
    try:
        m = re.search(r'\{.*\}', reply, re.S)
        obj = json.loads(m.group(0))
        if not isinstance(obj, dict):
            raise ValueError('not an object')
    except Exception as e:
        return 0.0, {'input': inp, 'reply': reply[:500], 'error': 'invalid JSON: ' + repr(e), 'failing_checks': ['json_parse']}
    per = {}
    detail = {}
    for k in KEYS:
        v = obj.get(k)
        if not isinstance(v, list):
            v = []
        got = [str(x).strip() for x in v]
        want = [str(x).strip() for x in exp.get(k, [])]
        per[k] = f1(got, want)
        detail[k] = {'expected': want, 'got': got, 'missing': sorted(set(want) - set(got)), 'extra': sorted(set(got) - set(want))}
    score = sum(per.values()) / len(KEYS)
    return score, {'input': inp, 'per_category_f1': per, 'detail': detail, 'failing_checks': [k for k in KEYS if per[k] < 1.0], 'reply': reply[:300]}
"
  },
  "objective": "Maximize mean per-category set F1 (person, org, location) of the JSON extracted from unseen sentences, scored by exact string match against gold. The prompt must make the model return only a JSON object with exactly the keys person, org, location (each a list of strings, empty list if none). Extra or hallucinated entities lower precision and are penalized by F1. Keep the prompt under 250 words.",
  "background": "Artifact is extract.txt, used as the system message; the sentence is the user message. Evaluator assumptions: an OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint reached via OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_BASE_URL (default https://api.openai.com/v1) and MODEL (default gpt-4o-mini), temperature 0. The seed prompt does not name the required keys, so it may score poorly at first. Gold conventions to preserve: titles are kept with names (e.g. 'Dr. Chen'), possessives are stripped ('NASA's' -> 'NASA'), the leading 'The' is dropped from org names ('Red Cross'), a single first name counts as a person, acronyms count as orgs, and a category with no entities is an empty list. Data is tiny (8 gold examples: 6 train, 2 held-out), so validation scores are noisy. Do not overfit to the specific names in the training examples.",
  "dataset": [
    {"input": "Priya Nair joined Stripe in Dublin last May.", "expected": {"person": ["Priya Nair"], "org": ["Stripe"], "location": ["Dublin"]}},
    {"input": "Acme Corp opened an office in Austin.", "expected": {"person": [], "org": ["Acme Corp"], "location": ["Austin"]}},
    {"input": "Nobody from Globex attended.", "expected": {"person": [], "org": ["Globex"], "location": []}},
    {"input": "Lena moved from Berlin to Lisbon.", "expected": {"person": ["Lena"], "org": [], "location": ["Berlin", "Lisbon"]}},
    {"input": "Initech hired Bill Lumbergh.", "expected": {"person": ["Bill Lumbergh"], "org": ["Initech"], "location": []}},
    {"input": "The Red Cross shipped supplies to Kyiv and Odesa.", "expected": {"person": [], "org": ["Red Cross"], "location": ["Kyiv", "Odesa"]}}
  ],
  "valset": [
    {"input": "Dr. Chen met Tomas Ruiz at the WHO in Geneva.", "expected": {"person": ["Dr. Chen", "Tomas Ruiz"], "org": ["WHO"], "location": ["Geneva"]}},
    {"input": "Sam Okafor spoke at NASA's Houston center.", "expected": {"person": ["Sam Okafor"], "org": ["NASA"], "location": ["Houston"]}}
  ],
  "config": {"max_metric_calls": 60, "max_iterations": 8}
}
```

train.f04:
Assumptions: `bench.py` takes the candidate path as `argv[1]`, so I'm using a shell evaluator with `{{candidate_file}}`. The score is already in [0,1], and it caps at 1.0 once the run is under 0.002s. That makes a small budget enough.

```json
{
  "seed_candidate": {
    "dedupe_py": "def dedupe(items):\n    out = []\n    for x in items:\n        if x not in out:\n            out.append(x)\n    return out\n"
  },
  "evaluator": {
    "type": "shell",
    "command": "python3 bench.py {{candidate_file}}",
    "score_pattern": "Score: ([0-9.]+)",
    "timeout": 60
  },
  "objective": "Maximize the bench.py score (target_time / runtime, capped at 1.0) for dedupe(items) on a 6000-element list of ints. The function must return a new list with duplicates removed, keeping the first occurrence of each item in original order. It must be correct on all bench cases, including the empty list and string items. Hard constraints: pure Python stdlib, define dedupe(items) at module level, no side effects, and don't mutate the input.",
  "background": "The seed is O(n^2) because it does a list membership test per item. bench.py exec's the candidate file and calls dedupe(), checks 4 small correctness cases, then times one call on 6000 random ints in 0..3000 against a 0.002s target. A wrong answer or a missing dedupe() scores 0. The score pattern matches 'Score: X' in the bench output. Idiomatic fast candidates are hash-based, e.g. list(dict.fromkeys(items)) or a seen-set loop. Assumption: items are hashable, as in the bench. If the candidate is meant to handle unhashable items such as lists or dicts, a try/except fallback to the slow path is acceptable, as long as the fast path stays first. The file must contain only the function definition, plus imports if needed. bench.py and dedupe.py are in the working directory and are not modified.",
  "config": {
    "max_metric_calls": 30,
    "max_iterations": 10
  }
}
```

After the run I'll fetch the best candidate and show you the diff and score gain. I won't overwrite `dedupe.py` until you agree.

train.f06:
Calling the optimizer with a shell evaluator that wraps `bench_anagram.py`. Assumptions are in `background`.

```json
{
  "seed_candidate": {
    "anagrams_py": "def group_anagrams(words):\n    groups = []\n    for w in words:\n        for g in groups:\n            if sorted(g[0]) == sorted(w):\n                g.append(w)\n                break\n        else:\n            groups.append([w])\n    return groups\n"
  },
  "evaluator": {
    "type": "shell",
    "command": "python3 bench_anagram.py {{candidate_file}}",
    "score_pattern": "Score: ([0-9.]+)",
    "timeout": 60
  },
  "objective": "Maximize the bench_anagram.py speed score (min(1, 0.003s / runtime) on 3000 random 4-letter words over 'abcdefg') for group_anagrams(words). Hard constraints: keep the function name and signature group_anagrams(words) -> list of lists of the original words; every word must appear in exactly one group, with words sharing a sorted-letter signature grouped together; preserve duplicates; stdlib only; no hardcoding of benchmark inputs, no caching across calls, and no special-casing the benchmark's alphabet or word length (it must work for arbitrary strings, including empty strings).",
  "background": "The current implementation is O(n * groups) because it compares sorted(g[0]) with sorted(w) for every existing group. A hash-based approach keyed on a canonical signature (e.g. a tuple or string of sorted letters) should reduce this to O(n * k log k). The benchmark execs the candidate file and calls group_anagrams. It checks correctness on a small sample, comparing normalized sorted groups, so group order and in-group order are not checked, but preferably keep first-seen group order and in-group input order. A wrong result gives Score 0. The benchmark prints 'Score: X' (capital S) in [0,1]. Timing is a single wall-clock measurement, so it is noisy, and the score caps at 1.0 once runtime is at or below 3 ms. Assumption: single-task mode, because the benchmark has one fixed workload. The file anagrams.py and bench_anagram.py are in the working directory, and the candidate is passed to the bench via a temp file path as argv[1]. The prod timeout is the motivating problem, so prefer algorithmic improvements over micro-hacks that would not generalize.",
  "config": {
    "max_metric_calls": 40,
    "max_iterations": 12
  }
}
```

train.f08:
This is a multi-task run on all 8 cases with no held-out set, since you didn't ask for generalization. The evaluator is a stdlib Python evaluator that runs the candidate's `parse_date` against each case and does an exact match.

```json
{
  "seed_candidate": {
    "dates_py": "from datetime import datetime

def parse_date(s):
    try:
        return datetime.strptime(s, '%Y-%m-%d').strftime('%Y-%m-%d')
    except ValueError:
        return None
"
  },
  "evaluator": {
    "type": "python",
    "code": "def evaluate(candidate, example):
    text = candidate if isinstance(candidate, str) else next(iter(candidate.values()), '')
    if example is None:
        example = {'input': '', 'expected': None}
    inp = example.get('input')
    expected = example.get('expected')
    try:
        ns = {}
        exec(compile(text, 'dates.py', 'exec'), ns)
        got = ns['parse_date'](inp)
    except Exception as e:
        return 0.0, {'input': inp, 'expected': expected, 'error': repr(e), 'failing_checks': ['exception_or_missing_parse_date']}
    try:
        ok = got == expected
        checks = []
        if not ok:
            if expected is None:
                checks.append('should_return_None_for_junk_or_invalid_date')
            elif got is None:
                checks.append('returned_None_for_valid_format')
            else:
                checks.append('wrong_date_value')
        return (1.0 if ok else 0.0), {'input': inp, 'expected': expected, 'got': got, 'failing_checks': checks}
    except Exception as e:
        return 0.0, {'input': inp, 'error': repr(e)}
",
    "timeout": 10
  },
  "objective": "Maximize the fraction of date_cases.json examples for which parse_date(input) exactly equals the expected output: a YYYY-MM-DD string for valid dates in the formats shown, and None for junk or invalid calendar dates (e.g. 31/02/2026). Hard constraints: Python standard library only, function named parse_date taking one string, never raises (return None on any failure), always returns either a zero-padded YYYY-MM-DD string or None.",
  "background": "Artifact is dates.py. Formats that must be handled: ISO (2026-11-11), slash numeric day/month/year (11/11/2026), abbreviated month with comma (Nov 11, 2026), day + full month name (11 November 2026), year/month/day with slashes (2026/03/05), and full month name without comma (March 5 2026). Junk ('not a date') and impossible dates (31/02/2026) must return None. Assumption: ambiguous numeric dates with slashes are interpreted as DD/MM/YYYY; the provided cases are consistent with either order because 11/11/2026 is symmetric and 31/02 is invalid in both, so this is a default choice. Prefer a general solution (try a list of strptime formats, strip whitespace, handle non-string input by returning None) over hardcoding the example strings. The candidate is exec'd as a module and parse_date is looked up in its namespace. Must keep `from datetime import datetime` or equivalent stdlib imports inside the file.",
  "dataset": [
    {"input": "2026-11-11", "expected": "2026-11-11"},
    {"input": "11/11/2026", "expected": "2026-11-11"},
    {"input": "Nov 11, 2026", "expected": "2026-11-11"},
    {"input": "11 November 2026", "expected": "2026-11-11"},
    {"input": "2026/03/05", "expected": "2026-03-05"},
    {"input": "March 5 2026", "expected": "2026-03-05"},
    {"input": "not a date", "expected": null},
    {"input": "31/02/2026", "expected": null}
  ],
  "config": {"max_metric_calls": 120, "max_iterations": 15}
}
```

train.f09:
I made these assumptions:
- **Seed:** you gave no existing regex, so I wrote a simple baseline.
- **Mode:** with only 6 examples and no request for held-out generalization, I used multi-task mode with all 6 as the dataset.
- **Matching:** the evaluator uses `re.match` from the start of the line, so the regex must carry its own anchors.

```json
{
  "seed_candidate": {
    "regex": "^(?P<ts>\S+) (?P<level>[A-Z]+) (?P<msg>.*)$"
  },
  "evaluator": {
    "type": "python",
    "code": "import re

def evaluate(candidate, example):
    text = candidate if isinstance(candidate, str) else next(iter(candidate.values()), '')
    if example is None:
        example = {'line': '2026-11-11T10:00:01Z INFO server started', 'want': {'ts': '2026-11-11T10:00:01Z', 'level': 'INFO', 'msg': 'server started'}}
    line = example.get('line', '')
    want = example.get('want')
    try:
        rx = re.compile(text.strip())
        m = rx.match(line)
    except Exception as e:
        return 0.0, {'line': line, 'error': repr(e), 'failing_checks': ['compile_or_match_error']}
    if want is None:
        ok = m is None
        return (1.0 if ok else 0.0), {'line': line, 'expected': None, 'got': (m.groupdict() if m else None), 'failing_checks': [] if ok else ['false_positive_non_log_line_matched']}
    if m is None:
        return 0.0, {'line': line, 'expected': want, 'got': None, 'failing_checks': ['no_match']}
    try:
        gd = m.groupdict()
    except Exception as e:
        return 0.0, {'line': line, 'error': repr(e)}
    per_field = {}
    failing = []
    for k, v in want.items():
        if k not in gd:
            per_field[k] = False
            failing.append('missing_group_' + k)
        else:
            good = gd[k] == v
            per_field[k] = good
            if not good:
                failing.append('wrong_' + k)
    score = sum(1 for x in per_field.values() if x) / max(len(per_field), 1)
    return float(score), {'line': line, 'expected': want, 'got': gd, 'per_field': per_field, 'failing_checks': failing}
",
    "timeout": 10
  },
  "objective": "Find a single regex with named groups ts, level, msg that matches every sample log line (ISO-8601 'T' timestamps with optional fractional seconds and Z, bracketed 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' timestamps without the brackets in ts, upper- or lowercase levels, variable whitespace after the level) and extracts exactly the expected ts, level and msg. Lines that are not logs must not match at all. Maximize the mean per-example score (1.0 = all fields exact, or no match on a non-log line). Hard constraints: Python re syntax only, must define groups named exactly ts, level and msg, no catastrophic backtracking, and no hard-coding of sample strings.",
  "background": "Artifact is a single Python `re` pattern, evaluated with re.match against each line (so include ^ and $ anchors as needed). Samples are in samples.jsonl as {line, want}; want=null means the line is not a log and must not match. Observed formats: (1) '<ISO ts>Z LEVEL msg' with optional fractional seconds, (2) '[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS] LEVEL msg' where ts excludes the brackets, (3) levels may be lowercase, (4) one or more spaces between level and msg, and msg must not include the leading padding. Assumptions: the level set is DEBUG/INFO/WARN/ERROR (case-insensitive; FATAL/TRACE/WARNING/CRITICAL are reasonable additions); inline flags such as (?i) are allowed. Only 6 samples exist and no held-out set was provided, so overfitting is possible: prefer general, structured patterns over per-sample tricks. Python 3 is assumed, with named groups using (?P<name>...). Duplicate group names across alternation branches are not allowed in `re`, so use a structure like an optional bracket wrapper around a single ts group.",
  "dataset": [
    {"line": "2026-11-11T10:00:01Z INFO server started", "want": {"ts": "2026-11-11T10:00:01Z", "level": "INFO", "msg": "server started"}},
    {"line": "2026-11-11T10:00:02Z WARN  disk 91% full", "want": {"ts": "2026-11-11T10:00:02Z", "level": "WARN", "msg": "disk 91% full"}},
    {"line": "[2026-11-11 10:00:03] ERROR db: timeout", "want": {"ts": "2026-11-11 10:00:03", "level": "ERROR", "msg": "db: timeout"}},
    {"line": "2026-11-11T10:00:04.123Z DEBUG cache miss key=a", "want": {"ts": "2026-11-11T10:00:04.123Z", "level": "DEBUG", "msg": "cache miss key=a"}},
    {"line": "garbage line", "want": null},
    {"line": "2026-11-11T10:00:05Z info lowercase level", "want": {"ts": "2026-11-11T10:00:05Z", "level": "info", "msg": "lowercase level"}}
  ],
  "config": {"max_metric_calls": 120, "max_iterations": 20}
}
```

train.f11:
I split the 11 examples 8 train / 3 held out, so the held-out check is noisy. The held-out three are one example per label, and the train set keeps the tricky refund-versus-damaged and shipping-versus-refund cases. I assumed an OpenAI-compatible endpoint configured through environment variables.

```json
{
  "seed_candidate": {
    "instructions": "Classify the customer message as shipping, refund, or damaged.
",
    "shots": "Message: where is my order
Label: shipping
"
  },
  "evaluator": {
    "type": "python",
    "timeout": 60,
    "code": "import json, os, re, urllib.request

LABELS = ['shipping', 'refund', 'damaged']

def evaluate(candidate, example):
    try:
        if isinstance(candidate, str):
            ins, shots = candidate, ''
        else:
            ins = candidate.get('instructions', '')
            shots = candidate.get('shots', '')
        if example is None:
            example = {'input': 'Where is my parcel?', 'expected': 'shipping'}
        system = (str(ins).strip() + '\n\n' + str(shots).strip()).strip()
        base = os.environ.get('OPENAI_BASE_URL', 'https://api.openai.com/v1').rstrip('/')
        model = os.environ.get('MODEL', 'gpt-4o-mini')
        key = os.environ.get('OPENAI_API_KEY', '')
        payload = {
            'model': model,
            'temperature': 0,
            'max_tokens': 20,
            'messages': [
                {'role': 'system', 'content': system},
                {'role': 'user', 'content': example['input']},
            ],
        }
        req = urllib.request.Request(
            base + '/chat/completions',
            data=json.dumps(payload).encode('utf-8'),
            headers={'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + key},
        )
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=40) as resp:
            data = json.loads(resp.read().decode('utf-8'))
        reply = data['choices'][0]['message']['content'] or ''
    except Exception as e:
        return 0.0, {'input': (example or {}).get('input', ''), 'error': repr(e)}
    low = reply.lower()
    found = [l for l in LABELS if re.search(r'\b' + l + r'\b', low)]
    expected = example['expected']
    if len(found) == 1:
        got = found[0]
    elif len(found) == 0:
        got = None
    else:
        got = 'AMBIGUOUS:' + ','.join(found)
    ok = got == expected
    checks = []
    if not ok:
        if got is None:
            checks.append('no_label_in_reply')
        elif str(got).startswith('AMBIGUOUS'):
            checks.append('multiple_labels_in_reply')
        else:
            checks.append('wrong_label')
    return (1.0 if ok else 0.0), {
        'input': example['input'],
        'expected': expected,
        'got': got,
        'raw_reply': reply[:200],
        'failing_checks': checks,
    }
"
  },
  "objective": "Maximize exact-match accuracy of the intent classifier (labels: shipping, refund, damaged) on held-out customer messages. The model must reply with exactly one label. Edit both the instructions and the few-shot block. Keep the total prompt concise (under about 300 words) and use only the three labels.",
  "background": "Assumptions: the evaluator calls an OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint via urllib, with OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_BASE_URL (default https://api.openai.com/v1) and MODEL (default gpt-4o-mini) read from the environment. The system message is the instructions text followed by the shots text; the user message is the customer message. Scoring is deterministic: the reply must contain exactly one of the three labels and it must match the expected one. Replies naming several labels score 0. The shots must be written as 'Message: ...\nLabel: ...' pairs. Labelling conventions in the data: a message about damage or defects is 'damaged' even if the customer also asks for money back or a replacement; a package that never arrived or is late is 'shipping' even if the customer asks for a refund; 'refund' is for money-back or cancel requests with no damage or delivery problem. The held-out set has only 3 examples, so the validation signal is noisy. Few-shot examples must be newly written and must not copy the dataset messages verbatim.",
  "dataset": [
    {"input": "Where's my package? It's been 9 days", "expected": "shipping"},
    {"input": "I want my money back", "expected": "refund"},
