train.f17:
I'm using an anchored LLM judge because there is no labeled data or A/B signal, and the target (persuasiveness toward one action) is subjective. This is single-task mode with a small budget. I don't know your product, so the background tells the optimizer to use placeholders instead of inventing features.

```json
{
  "seed_candidate": {
    "welcome_email": "Subject: Welcome\n\nThanks for signing up. Let us know if you have questions.\n"
  },
  "evaluator": {
    "type": "llm_judge",
    "criteria": "You are grading a new-user onboarding email whose only goal is to get the recipient to create their first project. Judge the email text (including the Subject line) against these criteria:\n\n1. Subject line: specific, under 50 characters, hints at a concrete benefit or next step. Generic subjects like 'Welcome' fail.\n2. Single primary CTA: exactly one clear, action-oriented call to action to create the first project (e.g. 'Create your first project'), placed early and repeated at most once. Multiple competing CTAs fail.\n3. Low friction: tells the reader how quick or easy the first step is (e.g. a time estimate or number of steps) without making claims that cannot be true for any product.\n4. Value: states in one or two sentences what they get from creating a project, in terms of outcome rather than features.\n5. Length and format: body 60-120 words, short paragraphs, plain text, no hype, no emojis, no ALL CAPS, no fake urgency.\n6. Honesty: no invented product facts, statistics, testimonials or discounts. Unknown specifics must be bracketed placeholders such as [Product], [link], [First name].\n7. Support: a brief, low-key offer of help (e.g. reply to this email) that does not compete with the main CTA.\n\nScore anchors: 10 = all criteria met; 8-9 = one minor miss (e.g. slightly long or subject a bit generic); 6-7 = two minor misses or the CTA is present but buried; 4 = a hard constraint is violated (no clear CTA, multiple competing CTAs, invented facts, or over 150 words); 1 = off-task or unchanged generic welcome.\n\nList violations as: criterion -> issue -> fix. End your response with exactly one line: SCORE: X/10",
    "timeout": 90
  },
  "objective": "Maximize the judge score for a welcome email that drives the recipient to create their first project: specific subject under 50 chars, one clear primary CTA, outcome-focused value statement, low-friction framing, 60-120 word plain-text body, no invented facts, brief offer of help. Keep the 'Subject:' line format.",
  "background": "Plain-text onboarding email (welcome.txt), sent right after signup. No A/B or conversion data exists, so the judge is a proxy for persuasiveness, not measured conversions; validate with a real A/B test after applying. The product is unknown from the file, so use bracketed placeholders ([Product], [First name], [link to create project]) and never invent features, stats, testimonials or offers. The user should fill in the placeholders and confirm the 'project' concept before sending. Must not change: it stays a plain-text email starting with a 'Subject:' line, and the goal remains first-project creation. Assumption: no length or brand-voice constraints were given beyond these defaults.",
