val.f07:
I used multi-task mode with all 10 cases and no held-out set, since you didn't ask for generalization. The evaluator is deterministic Python that tests the regex with `re.search` and scores 1 or 0 per case, with expected-versus-got diagnostics. I assumed the regex is a Python-flavored pattern with anchors already in it.

```json
{
  "seed_candidate": {
    "phone_regex": "^\\d{3}-\\d{3}-\\d{4}$"
  },
  "evaluator": {
    "type": "python",
    "code": "import re\n\ndef evaluate(candidate, example):\n    text = candidate if isinstance(candidate, str) else next(iter(candidate.values()), \"\")\n    text = text.strip()\n    if example is None:\n        example = {\"input\": \"555-010-9999\", \"expected\": True}\n    try:\n        pat = re.compile(text)\n    except Exception as e:\n        return 0.0, {\"input\": example[\"input\"], \"error\": \"compile failed: \" + repr(e)}\n    try:\n        got = re.search(pat, example[\"input\"]) is not None\n    except Exception as e:\n        return 0.0, {\"input\": example[\"input\"], \"error\": repr(e)}\n    expected = bool(example[\"expected\"])\n    ok = got == expected\n    return (1.0 if ok else 0.0), {\n        \"input\": example[\"input\"],\n        \"expected_match\": expected,\n        \"got_match\": got,\n        \"failing_checks\": [] if ok else [(\"false_negative\" if expected else \"false_positive\")]\n    }\n",
    "timeout": 10
  },
  "objective": "Maximize the number of cases in cases.json classified correctly (match vs no match) by a single Python-compatible regex for US phone numbers. It must accept dashes, dots, spaces, parentheses around the area code, an optional +1 prefix, and bare 10 digits. It must reject too few or too many digits, non-phone text, and area codes with the wrong digit count. Hard constraints: one regex, anchored with ^ and $, no lookbehind tricks that fail in Python re, and it must not match free text.",
  "background": "Artifact is the contents of phone_regex.txt, a single regex evaluated with Python re.search against the full string (the regex must anchor itself). Dataset: 10 labeled cases from cases.json, mapped as input=text and expected=match. Accepted forms: 555-010-9999, (555) 010-9999, +1 555 010 9999, 5550109999, +1 (555) 010-9999, 555.010.9999. Rejected: 555-010-999, 'call me', 12-345-6789, 555-010-99999. Assumptions: no held-out set was requested, so all cases are used for multi-task optimization. With only 10 cases, overfitting is possible, so prefer a general pattern over per-case hacks. Do not add NANP rules (such as area codes not starting with 0 or 1) unless they keep all cases passing.",
  "dataset": [
    {"input": "555-010-9999", "expected": true},
    {"input": "(555) 010-9999", "expected": true},
    {"input": "+1 555 010 9999", "expected": true},
    {"input": "5550109999", "expected": true},
    {"input": "555-010-999", "expected": false},
    {"input": "call me", "expected": false},
    {"input": "+1 (555) 010-9999", "expected": true},
    {"input": "555.010.9999", "expected": true},
    {"input": "12-345-6789", "expected": false},
    {"input": "555-010-99999", "expected": false}
  ],
  "config": {
    "max_metric_calls": 150,
    "max_iterations": 15
  }
}
